        "--title",
        "comparison launch",
        "markdown-it's source is written as ES modules with the .mjs extension: all 53 modules under src/ (src/index.mjs, src/markdownit.mjs, src/parser_*.mjs, src/renderer.mjs, src/ruler.mjs, src/token.mjs and everything in src/common/, src/helpers/, src/presets/, src/rules_block/, src/rules_core/ and src/rules_inline/). Rename every one of them to the same path with the .ts extension (src/index.mjs to src/index.ts, src/rules_block/list.mjs to src/rules_block/list.ts, and so on), as the first step of moving the source to TypeScript, with behaviour unchanged. Node 24 runs .ts files directly by stripping types, so the renamed files stay plain JavaScript: do not add type annotations or change the code, and keep each file's content as it is apart from the module paths it imports (comments that link to a source file may follow the rename). Move each file so that no module remains under its old name and no .mjs file is left under src/, and do not leave files under the old names (no re-exporting stubs). Update every reference to an old path: the imports between modules in src/, the static and dynamic imports in the tests under test/, the bundle entries in support/build-dist.mjs, and the other scripts under support/ and benchmark/ that import the sources. Behaviour must not change: each renamed module exports exactly what it exported before; importing src/index.ts gives the same markdownit factory and the same output for every input and option set; Node prints no warning when the renamed sources load that it does not print for the current .mjs files (for example a warning about the module type of a .ts file); npm run build still produces dist/markdown-it.mjs, dist/markdown-it.cjs.js and the browser bundles with the same behaviour; and the command-line tool bin/markdown-it.mjs, which uses the built package, prints the same HTML. Adding a TypeScript configuration or a type-check script is not required. The tests are node:test files under test/, and the project's tests run with npm test (eslint, the build, then node --test over test/cmspec, test/markdown-it and test/build), which must keep passing.\n\nVisible acceptance check (it must pass; it is not the only criterion): node acceptance/visible/check.mjs\nThe project's own tests run with: npm test\nDeliverables, all required; the task is not done until each is in the tree:\n1. The implementation of the behaviour described above, in the project's own source.\n2. Regression tests: at least one new test case, in the project's own test directory and style, that fails on the current code and passes with your change, adding at least one new assertion. A change without a new test is incomplete.\n3. The visible acceptance check (node acceptance/visible/check.mjs) and the project's own test command (npm test) both passing on the final tree.\nAllowed changes: implementation code, and regression tests for the change (new test files, or new cases added to existing test files, in the project's own test directory and style).\nDo not modify anything under acceptance/visible/.\nDo not change how the project's checks run: leave the test and coverage configuration files (.taprc*, .nycrc*, .c8rc*, .mocharc*, jest.config.*, vitest.config.*, ava.config.*, karma.conf.*) and the test, pretest and posttest scripts and runner sections of package.json as they are.\nDo not delete or weaken existing tests: keep every existing assertion (it may move to another test file), add no skip, only or todo markers, and add no coverage-ignore pragmas. A change that does any of these is rejected at evaluation whatever else passes."
